Biographies of 張釋之 and 馮唐

The廷尉 張釋之 was a man of 堵陽, his courtesy name was 季. He had an older brother 仲 with whom he lived. By means of property he became a 騎郎, serving 孝文帝. For ten years he could not be transferred, and he was not known. 釋之 said, "Long serving in office diminishes 仲's property, and is unsuccessful." He wanted to excuse himself and return home. The中郎將 袁盎 knew his worth and was pained by his departure, so he requested to transfer 釋之 to fill the post of 謁者. After 釋之 had finished his court audience, he thereupon spoke of matters of practical advantage. 文帝 said, "Lower it, do not make very high theories, so that it can be implemented today." Thereupon 釋之 spoke for a long time about the events between 秦 and 漢, and the reasons why 秦 lost and 漢 rose. 文帝 praised it, and thus appointed 釋之 as 謁者仆射.

釋之 followed on an excursion, and ascended the tiger enclosure. The Emperor asked the上林尉 about the registers of the various birds and beasts; after more than ten questions, the尉 looked left and right, and was entirely unable to answer. The虎圈嗇夫 from the side answered for the尉 what the Emperor asked about the bird and beast registers very thoroughly, wishing thereby to show his ability to answer orally and respond without end. 文帝 said, "Should not an official be like this? The尉 is useless!" He then ordered 釋之 to appoint the嗇夫 as 上林令. 釋之 stepped forward after a long time and said, "Your Majesty, what kind of person do you consider 絳侯 周勃 to be?" The Emperor said, "A長者." He asked again, "What kind of person is 東陽侯 張相如?" The Emperor again said, "A長者." 釋之 said, "絳侯 and 東陽侯 are called長者; these two men, when speaking of affairs, could not even get the words out of their mouths. How could they imitate this嗇夫's chattering, sharp mouth and quick replies! Moreover, 秦 employed刀筆之吏; the officials competed to be swift, harsh, and scrutinizing to elevate themselves above one another, but their flaw was merely that it was outward forms without the reality of compassion. For this reason, they did not hear of their own faults, and declined until by the time of 二世, the empire crumbled like dirt. Now Your Majesty, because of the嗇夫's eloquence, promotes him extraordinarily. I fear the empire will follow this trend, competing to be eloquent without having the substance. Moreover, the transformation of those below by those above is faster than shadow and echo; your actions cannot but be examined carefully." 文帝 said, "Good." He then stopped and did not appoint the嗇夫.

The Emperor got into his carriage, summoned 釋之 to ride as參乘, and going slowly, asked 釋之 about the flaws of 秦. He told him completely with plain words. When they arrived at the palace, the Emperor appointed 釋之 as 公車令.

Shortly after, the太子 and 梁王 rode together in a carriage to enter the court, and did not dismount at the司馬門. Thereupon 釋之 pursued and stopped the太子 and 梁王, so that they could not enter the palace gates. He then impeached them for not dismounting at the public gates as disrespectful, and memorialized it. 薄太后 heard of this. 文帝 took off his cap and apologized, saying, "I have not been careful in teaching my sons." 薄太后 then sent an envoy bearing an edict to pardon the太子 and 梁王, and only then were they allowed to enter. 文帝 from this considered 釋之 extraordinary, and appointed him as 中大夫.

Shortly after, he became 中郎將. He followed on an excursion to 霸陵, where they were situated on the north overlooking the toilet. At this time 慎夫人 was in attendance. The Emperor pointed out to 慎夫人 the road to 新豐, saying, "This is the road that leads to 邯鄲." He had 慎夫人 play the瑟, and the Emperor himself leaned on the瑟 and sang. His mood was miserable and sorrowful. He looked back and said to the ministers, "Alas! If one uses the stones of the north mountain as a coffin, uses cut ramie and cotton floss to line it, and lacquers the spaces between, could it be moved!" The attendants all said, "Good." 釋之 stepped forward and said, "If there is something desirable inside, even if it is sealed with the south mountain, there will still be cracks; if there is nothing desirable inside, even if there is no stone coffin, what sorrow would there be!" 文帝 praised it. Later he appointed 釋之 as 廷尉.

Shortly after, the Emperor went out and traveled over the中渭橋. A man ran out from under the bridge, and the horses of the imperial carriage were startled. Thereupon he sent riders to arrest him, and handed him over to the廷尉. 釋之 examined and questioned him. The man said, "I came from the county, heard the clearing of the road, and hid under the bridge. After a long time, thinking the procession had passed, I came out, saw the imperial carriage and riders, and just ran." The廷尉 memorialized the sentence: a commoner committed the offense of violating the road clearing, and the sentence should be a fine. 文帝 angrily said, "This man personally startled my horses. My horses were luckily gentle; if it had been other horses, would they not surely have thrown and injured me? Yet the廷尉 sentences him to a fine!" 釋之 said, "The law is what the Son of Heaven shares in common with the world. Now the law is thus, but if you make it heavier, then the law will not be trusted by the people. Moreover, at that time, if Your Majesty had immediately executed him, it would have been done. Now that he has been handed down to the廷尉, the廷尉 is the balance of the world. If it tips once, then the application of the law throughout the world will become light or heavy [arbitrary]. How will the people know where to place their hands and feet? I only hope Your Majesty examines this." After a long while, the Emperor said, "The廷尉's sentence is correct."

Later, someone stole a jade ring from in front of the seat in the高廟. He was caught. 文帝 was angry and handed him down to the廷尉 to be judged. 釋之, according to the law that one who steals the imperial utensils of the ancestral temple should be memorialized, memorialized that the sentence should be execution in the marketplace. The Emperor was greatly angered and said, "This man is without the Way, to actually steal the temple vessels of the late Emperor. The reason I handed him to the廷尉 was that I wanted to exterminate his family, but you memorialize according to the law; this is not my intention in reverently maintaining the ancestral temple." 釋之 took off his cap, knocked his head on the ground, and apologized, saying, "The law being thus is sufficient. Moreover, for equal crimes, the difference is based on whether they are contrary or compliant. Now, to exterminate the family of one who steals ancestral temple vessels—if there is a one-in-ten-thousand chance that a foolish person takes a handful of earth from 長陵, how would Your Majesty increase the law for that?" After a long time, 文帝 spoke of it with the太后, and then approved the廷尉's sentence. At this time, the中尉 條侯 周亞夫 and the梁相 山都侯 王恬開 saw that 釋之 upheld his arguments fairly, and so they became close friends with him. 廷尉 張 was from this praised by the world.

Later 文帝 died, and 景帝 ascended the throne. 釋之 was afraid, and claimed to be ill. He wanted to resign and leave, but feared a great execution would arrive; he wanted to appear and apologize, but did not know how it would go. He used the plan of 王生, eventually appeared and apologized, and 景帝 did not hold it against him.

王生 was good at speaking of 黃老, and was a處士. He was once summoned to stay at the court. The三公 and 九卿 were all gathered and standing. 王生, an old man, said, "My sock has come undone," and looking back, said to 廷尉 張, "Tie my sock for me!" 釋之 knelt and tied it. After this was done, someone said to 王生, "Why did you publicly humiliate 廷尉 張, making him kneel and tie your sock?" 王生 said, "I am old and lowly, and I estimate that in the end I will be of no benefit to 廷尉 張. 廷尉 張 is now a famous minister of the world. I therefore purposely humiliated the廷尉, making him kneel and tie my sock, in order to elevate him." The various lords heard of this, and considered 王生 worthy and respected 廷尉 張 even more.

廷尉 張 served 景帝 for a little over a year, and became the相 of 淮南王, still because of his former offense. After a long time, 釋之 died. His son was named 張摯, courtesy name 長公. His office reached 大夫, and he was dismissed. Because he could not accommodate himself to the times, he did not serve in office for the rest of his life.

馮唐: his grandfather was a man of 趙. His father moved to 代. When 漢 rose, they moved to 安陵. 唐 was noted for filial piety, and became 中郎署長, serving 文帝. 文帝 passed by in a carriage and asked 唐, "Old father, how did you come to be a郎? Where is your home?" 唐 answered completely with the truth. 文帝 said, "When I lived in 代, my尚食監 高袪 often spoke to me of the worthiness of the 趙 general 李齊, who fought below 鉅鹿. Now every time I eat, my thoughts have never been absent from 鉅鹿. Do you know of him?" 唐 replied, "He is still not as good as 廉頗 and 李牧 as generals." The Emperor said, "Why?" 唐 said, "When my grandfather was in 趙, he was a官率將, and was a good friend of 李牧. My father was formerly the相 of 代, and was a good friend of the 趙 general 李齊, and knew his character." The Emperor, having heard the character of 廉頗 and 李牧, was very pleased, and slapping his thigh, said, "Alas! If I could only get 廉頗 and 李牧 to be my generals at this time, how could I worry about the 匈奴!" 唐 said, "Your servant humbly says: even if Your Majesty got 廉頗 and 李牧, you could not use them." The Emperor was angry, rose, and entered the forbidden chambers. After a long while, he summoned 唐 and rebuked him, saying, "Why did you publicly humiliate me? Was there no private place?" 唐 apologized, saying, "A rustic man does not know taboos."

At this time, the 匈奴 had newly greatly invaded 朝那, and killed the 北地都尉 卬. The Emperor was concerned about the 胡 bandits, and so he finally asked 唐 again, saying, "How do you know I could not use 廉頗 and 李牧?" 唐 replied, "Your servant has heard that when the kings of high antiquity dispatched generals, they would kneel and push the wheel, saying, 'What is within the gates, I control; what is outside the gates, the general controls.' Military merit, ranks, and rewards were all decided on the outside, and reported upon return. This is not an empty saying. My grandfather said that when 李牧 was a 趙 general stationed at the border, the taxes from the military markets were all used by himself to feast the soldiers; rewards were decided on the outside, and not interfered with from within. He delegated responsibility and demanded success, so 李牧 was able to exhaust his intelligence and ability. He dispatched and selected 1,300 chariots, 13,000 crossbowmen, and 100,000 warriors of a hundred gold, thereby driving back the單于 in the north, defeating 東胡, destroying 澹林, suppressing strong 秦 in the west, and supporting 韓 and 魏 in the south. At that time, 趙 was nearly a hegemon. Later, it happened that 趙王遷 ascended the throne; his mother was an entertainer. When 王遷 ascended the throne, he used the slander of 郭開, and finally executed 李牧, ordering 顏聚 to replace him. As a result, the army was defeated and the soldiers fled, and they were captured and destroyed by 秦. Now your servant has privately heard that 魏尚 is the守 of 雲中. The taxes from his military markets are entirely used to feast the soldiers; he takes out his private money, kills a cow every five days, and feasts his guests, military officers, and retainers. For this reason, the 匈奴 avoid him from afar and do not approach the 雲中 border. The enemy once entered, and 尚 led the chariots and riders to attack them, killing a great number of them. Now, the soldiers are all sons of common families; they rise from the fields to join the army, so how would they know the foot-long registers and five-man tallies? They fight with all their might all day, behead enemies and capture prisoners, and report the merits to the headquarters. If one word does not match, the clerks use the law to bind them. Their rewards are not carried out, but the clerks strictly apply the law. Your servant is foolish, and thinks that Your Majesty's laws are too strict, rewards are too light, and punishments are too heavy. Moreover, the 雲中守 魏尚 was impeached because the heads and captives reported for merit were short by six ranks. Your Majesty handed him down to the clerks, stripped him of his rank, and punished him with labor. From this it can be said that even if Your Majesty got 廉頗 and 李牧, you could not use them. Your servant is truly foolish, and has touched upon a taboo. Death penalty, death penalty!" 文帝 was pleased. That day he ordered 馮唐 to hold the tally and pardon 魏尚, restoring him as 雲中守, and appointed 唐 as 車騎都尉, in charge of the中尉 and the chariot soldiers of the commanderies and kingdoms.

In the seventh year, 景帝 ascended the throne, and made 唐 the相 of 楚, but he was dismissed. 武帝 ascended the throne, sought the worthy and good, and recommended 馮唐. 唐 was then over ninety years old, and could no longer serve as an official. So he made 唐's son 馮遂 a郎. 遂, courtesy name 王孫, was also an extraordinary man, and was good friends with me.

The太史公 says: 張季's words about the長者, upholding the law and not flattering intentions; 馮公's discourse on generals, how flavorful it is! How flavorful it is! The saying goes, "If you do not know the man, look at his friends." What these two lords praised and recited could be displayed in the ancestral temple and court. The書 says, "Without partiality or partisanship, the King's Way is broad; without partisanship or partiality, the King's Way is level." 張季 and 馮公 came close to this. [end of text]


